 By contrast, a smooth surface lesion, this can be on the facial surface near the gumline,

222
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:45,080
 but most commonly it's on the musular distal surface right up against an adjacent tooth.

223
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:50,960
 This is the opposite situation, it starts out wide and narrows out until it reaches the

224
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:53,520
 dentin enamel junction.

225
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:58,720
 At which point dentin is a lot easier to penetrate than enamel, so it spreads out again, kind

226
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:06,240
 of forms this double arrowhead and then penetrates deeper and deeper until it gets treated.

227
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:12,120
 And lastly we have root surface lesions over here on the right, and as we talked about,

228
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:17,160
 they can progress rapidly because there's no enamel, no it's a V shape, just like this

229
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:22,680
 smooth surface lesion, as the root surface is also a smooth surface.

230
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:29,200
 And again, we talked about the critical pH being much higher, hence why it can progress

231
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:33,040
 so much quicker.

232
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:40,360
 We also have to distinguish between infected dentin and a-fected dentin.

233
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:48,120
 So infected dentin refers to this superficial, wet, soft, mushy, necrotic dentin.

234
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:53,460
 That's this stuff, it's easily removed and it has to be removed, because bacteria are

235
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:58,520
 present there, this is a site of active infection.

236
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:06,080
 Whereas a-fected dentin is deeper, it's dry, it's leathery, it's demineralized, but not

237
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:08,800
 invaded by bacteria.

238
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:13,280
 So that's a little bit deeper in this cavity preparation.

239
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:19,120
 You can also see how they're distinguished in this awesome diagram, we have infected

240
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:22,760
 dentin on top, and then a-fected dentin down here.

241
00:22:22,760 --> 00:22:27,240
 You also get some sclerotic dentin, that's where the dentin is a little bit harder and

242
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:32,720
 tries to resist the passage of carries deeper and deeper into the tooth.

243
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:40,360
 And a-fected dentin is a-fected by bacterial toxins and acids, but again the actual bacteria

244
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:42,560
 have not made a home there yet.

245
00:22:42,560 --> 00:22:48,680
 So it's usually okay to leave a-fected dentin at the base of your prep, particularly if

246
00:22:48,680 --> 00:22:55,120
 you're close to the pulp and you're worried about exposing it.

247
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:58,200
 So let's talk a little bit more about progress of lesions.

248
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:04,440
 We can have an intact surface, which is essential for that remineralization process.

249
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:11,600
 So we need that enamel to be intact for there to be remineralization, the equilibrium driving

250
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:14,040
 to the left.

251
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:20,880
 Cavitation, a nick or a hole, is an irreversible process that now requires restorative treatment.

252
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:27,880
 We cannot remineralize a cavitation back to a smooth intact surface.

253
00:23:27,880 --> 00:23:31,480
 Cavitation is irreversible.

254
00:23:31,480 --> 00:23:33,800
 And like I said, this takes time.

255
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:40,400
 It may take one to two years to form an actual enamel cavitation or cavity.

256
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:44,280
 So from a white spot to a cavitation can take one to two years.

257
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:46,280
 That's a long, long time.

258
00:23:46,280 --> 00:23:54,040
 So that's this tug of war between remineralization and demineralization occurring over years and

259
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:57,600
 the demineralization finally winning.

260
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:05,160
 So the order, and I have seen this as a board exam question by itself, is enamel demonization

261
00:24:05,160 --> 00:24:06,160
 first.

262
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,400
 The surface remains intact.

263
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:11,080
 Then we get dentin demineralization.

264
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:19,880
 So start to see just little evidences of these tiny little demineralized regions of dentin.

265
00:24:19,880 --> 00:24:22,720
 And then we get that enamel cavitation.

266
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:29,840
 So this is our irreversible stage where that we can actually feel a cavity there.

267
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:36,040
 And then we get dentin cavitation where that cavity progresses much, much more quickly at

268
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:38,200
 that point.

269
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:44,720
 So enamel demon, dentin demin, enamel cavitation, dentin cavitation.

270
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:49,920
 That is the order of a cavity.

271
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:52,040
 So some really good terms to know.

272
00:24:52,040 --> 00:24:58,560
 We're going to talk about Keri's terms with regards to extent of the lesion first.

273
00:24:58,560 --> 00:25:08,080
 So an incipient lesion or a reversible lesion occurs when it occurs on a smooth surface.

274
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:11,520
 You're going to notice this very specific quality.

275
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:17,800
 It appears opaque white when it's air dried, but it seems to go away when it's wet.

276
00:25:17,800 --> 00:25:22,040
 So we'll talk about that a little bit more in the next video when we talk about the clinical

277
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:25,920
 exam, how we look for Keri's and detect them.

278
00:25:25,920 --> 00:25:30,400
 But that is an important thing to know for the incipient Keri's lesion.

279
00:25:30,400 --> 00:25:35,920
 So if we're talking about incipient, we're kind of in this enamel demon, maybe dentin

280
00:25:35,920 --> 00:25:42,680
 demon, there's some controversy there, but we're certainly not at the cavitation point.

281
00:25:42,680 --> 00:25:50,480
 So incipient, we're in this safe zone where we can reverse the process back to normal.

282
00:25:50,480 --> 00:25:56,600
 And then on the flip side, we have the cavitated or irreversible lesion where the enamel surface

283
00:25:56,600 --> 00:26:02,000
 is broken, it's no longer intact, and usually the lesion has advanced into dentin.

284
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:08,280
 Again, we see now we're on this half, so we're starting off in incipient lesion territory,

285
00:26:08,280 --> 00:26:13,200
 we can reverse it, then at this point, we're no longer reversible.

286
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:16,440
 So that's incipient and cavitated.

287
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:20,440
 Now let's talk about Keri's terms with regards to the location.

288
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:21,680
 Really good stuff here.

289
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:28,120
 A simple cavity, simple Keri's lesion, covers one surface of a tooth.

290
00:26:28,120 --> 00:26:34,880
 Also occlusal is the most common simple lesion you'll find on a posterior tooth.

291
00:26:34,880 --> 00:26:38,920
 If you're curious, this is also true for a restoration.

292
00:26:38,920 --> 00:26:46,120
 So restorations are also given, they can also be classified by these first three terms here.

293
00:26:46,120 --> 00:26:50,320
 So simple one surface, compound is two surfaces.

294
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:53,640
 So mesial occlusal, distal occlusal.

295
00:26:53,640 --> 00:26:58,920
 Complex means it covers three or more surfaces of the tooth.

296
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:04,520
 So MOD, or it could be MODFL, it could be all five surfaces of a tooth.

297
00:27:04,520 --> 00:27:10,520
 So this is Keri's terms with regards to how many surfaces that cavity is affecting, and

298
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:15,680
 the restoration to fix that can also be defined by those same terms.

299
00:27:15,680 --> 00:27:19,080
 Down here we have the primary Keri's lesion.

300
00:27:19,080 --> 00:27:23,680
 A primary lesion is the original Keri's lesion.

301
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:28,480
 So in other words, it's the first time lesion on that surface.

302
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:33,680
 You have a virgin tooth that's never been affected by a cavity before.

303
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:40,440
 You notice a cavity on the occlusal surface of it, that's a primary lesion.

304
00:27:40,440 --> 00:27:48,320
 Now if you had a tooth that already had a restoration, let's say on the mesial surface,

305
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:54,240
 but it never had anything on the distal surface, and the dentist notices that the distal surface

306
00:27:54,240 --> 00:28:00,120
 now is decay, that distal surface is also a primary lesion, because it's the first time

307
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:03,800
 that surface got decay.

308
00:28:03,800 --> 00:28:09,880
 In contrast, we have the secondary or recurrent Keri's lesion, and this occurs at the junction

309
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:17,320
 of a tooth and a restoration indicating micro leakage underneath that restoration.

310
00:28:17,320 --> 00:28:22,920
 There is marginal gap, bacteria got in there, so it created that lactic acid and created

311
00:28:22,920 --> 00:28:24,520
 another cavity.

312
00:28:24,520 --> 00:28:31,240
 So it's Keri's that's born from another lesion that had already been restored, but now we

313
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:34,080
 have decay underneath it.

314
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:39,160
 This is important to distinguish from residual, which kind of, it seems like the same thing

315
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:42,280
 at first, but it's distinctly different.

316
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:48,840
 So residual Keri's residual lesion refers to Keri's that remain in a completed tooth

317
00:28:48,840 --> 00:28:50,040
 prep.

318
00:28:50,040 --> 00:28:56,320
 So that means that we did the preparation and immediately after that preparation's done,

319
00:28:56,320 --> 00:29:01,880
 there still Keri's there, that lesion was not completely removed.

320
00:29:01,880 --> 00:29:07,720
 Sometimes that's done on purpose, but most of the time it's not, and it's an unfortunate

321
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:15,640
 thing when that happens that Keri's was left behind, and that is residual Keri's.

322
00:29:15,640 --> 00:29:21,240
 Recurrent Keri's occurs when that restoration was done, Keri's was completely removed, and

323
00:29:21,240 --> 00:29:27,040
 then at a later date Keri's now affected that area.

324
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:30,080
 So some really good location terms.

325
00:29:30,080 --> 00:29:37,840
 Let's finish this off with Keri's terms that have to do with the rate of the decay progression.

326
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:43,360
 So we have up here a cute or rampant Keri's, this is where you get rapid damage to the

327
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:49,960
 tooth structure, it's lightly colored, it feels soft, and it's infectious.

328
00:29:49,960 --> 00:29:55,800
 In contrast, chronic or slow Keri's means we have de-mineralized tooth structure that's

329
00:29:55,800 --> 00:30:02,200
 almost remineralized, it's discolored, it's fairly hard, it's progressing at a slower rate.

330
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:08,440
 That tug of war is not so one-sided like it was with acute, it's a little bit more balanced,

331
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:15,120
 we're getting a little bit more remineralization to help fight off that progression.

332
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:21,020
 And then we can have arrested lesions that appear brown or black, they're hard to the

333
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:25,600
 touch, and their Keri's resistant if exposed to fluoride.

334
00:30:25,600 --> 00:30:31,720
 So this is a cavity that has completely, has been arrested completely, the equilibrium

335
00:30:31,720 --> 00:30:36,840
 is not moving in the direction of de-mineralization, which is a good thing.

336
00:30:36,840 --> 00:30:43,280
 And if it's in the dentin, we get that sclerotic dentin, that's that hard dentin that is laid

337
00:30:43,280 --> 00:30:52,000
 down by the odonna blast to protect the tooth from further ingress of bacteria.

338
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:57,400
 So if we look down here, which of these looks worse to you?

339
00:30:57,400 --> 00:31:01,400
 And maybe at first glance you'd say, well, the one on the right looks darker and just

340
00:31:01,400 --> 00:31:05,200
 more formidable in some ways.

341
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:10,640
 But knowing what you know now, that's actually an arrested lesion.

342
00:31:10,640 --> 00:31:11,640
 And so that's a good thing.

343
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:15,640
 And if you examine that with an explorer, it would be hard to the touch, whereas the

344
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:22,360
 left one would be soft, and that is an active rampant Keri's lesion.

345
00:31:22,360 --> 00:31:27,440
 That's an active Keri's lesion, it's actively progressing, and that one certainly needs

346
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:28,920
 to be restored.

347
00:31:28,920 --> 00:31:34,440
 The arrested one, maybe if they're not, if you're not concerned with the overall health

348
00:31:34,440 --> 00:31:41,280
 of the tooth, there's no pulp exposure, it's sound, there's no risk of fracture, it could

349
00:31:41,280 --> 00:31:46,000
 be left alone unless the aesthetics bothers the patient, but of course it would be best

350
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:51,280
 to restore that as well because that's just a retentive area for food and plaque to get

351
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:54,320
 in there.

352
00:31:54,320 --> 00:32:00,800
 So in the periodontic series, we talked about the different microbiological hypotheses for

353
00:32:00,800 --> 00:32:02,440
 gum disease.

354
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:03,920
 For Keri's, it's pretty simple.

355
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:09,400
 We abide primarily by the specific plaque hypothesis, where only specific bacteria in

356
00:32:09,400 --> 00:32:15,080
 the oral cavity cause Keri's, and those are carogenic bacteria.

357
00:32:15,080 --> 00:32:21,240
 So let's talk about three specific bacteria that are most intimately related with dental

358
00:32:21,240 --> 00:32:23,000
 Keri's.

359
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:31,600
 The big one, Streptococcus mutans, this one is the big bad for enamel Keri's, it's a

360
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:41,480
 gram-positive coccye, and it produces glucoseal transferase, or GTF, which converts sucrose,

361
00:32:41,480 --> 00:32:46,160
 which is kind of a main sugar from most of our foods that we're eating.

362
00:32:46,160 --> 00:32:52,520
 It converts that to glucans and fructoseans, which are extracellular polysaccharides that

363
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:56,240
 help the bacteria stick to the tooth.

364
00:32:56,240 --> 00:33:03,520
 So these glucans and fructoseans are kind of like slime and this sticky stuff that helps

365
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:06,240
 the bacteria adhere to the tooth.

366
00:33:06,240 --> 00:33:10,080
 So really bad for us, good for the bacteria.

367
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:13,080
 It's also acidogenic and aciduric.

368
00:33:13,080 --> 00:33:19,360
 We know it's acidogenic, which means it converts the sucrose into lactic acid via fermentation,

369
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:23,400
 which we talked about in our big equilibrium diagram.

370
00:33:23,400 --> 00:33:29,760
 And also it's aciduric, it's durable in acid, it tolerates the acid well.

371
00:33:29,760 --> 00:33:37,200
 So it can bathe in its own secretion, so to speak, and it doesn't get damaged or killed

372
00:33:37,200 --> 00:33:40,200
 from having low pH, it actually likes it.

373
00:33:40,200 --> 00:33:45,800
 And it also produces bacteria sins, which means it can kill off competing microbes.

374
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:54,600
 So this thing is nasty, and it is definitely the main cause of enamel carries, and certainly

375
00:33:54,600 --> 00:34:02,440
 a really, really important one to know for the board exam and for life moving forward.

376
00:34:02,440 --> 00:34:10,600
 We also have lactobacillus, which is the primary cause of dentinol carries and actinomyces,

377
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:14,080
 which is the primary cause for root carries.

378
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:19,520
 So this is a bit of an oversimplification, but I definitely think it's helpful to know

379
00:34:19,520 --> 00:34:20,520
 for the board exam.

380
00:34:20,520 --> 00:34:26,200
 It just kind of keeps it easy, and you just have to think of one bacteria for one region

381
00:34:26,200 --> 00:34:31,920
 or one zone of the tooth that's affected by carries.

382
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:35,600
 And so I've talked a lot about what can go wrong in the carries process.

383
00:34:35,600 --> 00:34:41,320
 We also touched on remineralization, and so as you know, the main natural protective element

384
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:46,840
 is saliva, so saliva can come to the rescue, of course, brushing your teeth is the best

385
00:34:46,840 --> 00:34:52,080
 thing you can do, but saliva contains a lot of great stuff to help us out.

386
00:34:52,080 --> 00:34:59,600
 So it has glycoproteins up here, these large molecules that can agglutinate bacteria together,

387
00:34:59,600 --> 00:35:04,920
 just glue them and stick them all together to help eliminate them through swallowing.

388
00:35:04,920 --> 00:35:11,280
 So they get stuck altogether and get swallowed with a bolus of food, urea and other buffers,

389
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:16,400
 like bicarbonate that we talked about, dilutes the bacterial acid byproducts, helps drive

390
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:22,840
 the reaction to the right, it contains lysosine, which destroys cell walls, so that's really

391
00:35:22,840 --> 00:35:30,800
 useful, it can kill bacteria, damage them, lactofarin, actively binds iron, which is

392
00:35:30,800 --> 00:35:38,520
 important for bacterial enzymes, so it steals away the iron that the bacteria like to use,

393
00:35:38,520 --> 00:35:47,880
 iron that ferris that's coming in here, and lacto, I like to remember, act from lacto

394
00:35:47,880 --> 00:35:56,080
 inactivate, and then iron, of course, related to this stem of the word, so lactofarin in

395
00:35:56,080 --> 00:36:04,000
 activates iron, and then we have lactoparoxidase, which inactivates bacterial enzymes, so again,

396
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:11,580
 we have act here, so we're inactivating aces, which are enzymes, so that's how I keep these

397
00:36:11,580 --> 00:36:16,160
 two things straight and they almost always pop up on the board exam, so just a little

398
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:19,000
 helpful memory tip there.

399
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:25,520
 saliva also contains salivary IgA, which is a menoglobulin A, it's a salivary antibody

400
00:36:25,520 --> 00:36:33,000
 that's specifically made against bacteria in the oral cavity, of course saliva is also

401
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:37,080
 containing calcium, phosphate, and fluoride ions that help with remineralization, you

402
00:36:37,080 --> 00:36:42,720
 know all about that by now, and also contains these other things that can help promote the

403
00:36:42,720 --> 00:36:45,240
 remineralization process.

